'''''Avengers Assemble''''' is an American [[Superhero fiction|superhero]] [[animated series]] based on the [[Marvel Comics]] superhero team known as the [[Avengers (comics)|Avengers]]. Designed to capitalize on the success of the 2012 film ''[[The Avengers (2012 film)|The Avengers]]'', the series premiered on [[Disney XD]] on May 26, 2013,<ref name="Airdate"/><ref name="Guide">{{cite web|url=http://www.tvguide.com/news/exclusive-marvel-assembles-1048788.aspx |title=Exclusive: Marvel Assembles New Animated Series for the Hulk and Avengers |access-date=July 5, 2012 |date=June 12, 2012 |work=[[TV Guide]] |last=Sands |first=Rich |archive-date=September 11, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120911013525/http://www.tvguide.com/News/Exclusive-Marvel-Assembles-1048788.aspx |url-status=live}}</ref> as the successor to ''[[The Avengers: Earth's Mightiest Heroes]]''.

It previously aired alongside ''[[Ultimate Spider-Man (TV series)|Ultimate Spider-Man]]'', ''[[Hulk and the Agents of S.M.A.S.H.]]'', and ''[[Guardians of the Galaxy (TV series)|Guardians of the Galaxy]]'' as a part of the ''[[Disney XD#Current|Marvel Universe]]'' block. [[Joe Casey]], [[Joe Kelly (comics writer)|Joe Kelly]], [[Duncan Rouleau]] and [[Steven T. Seagle]], known collectively as ''[[Man of Action Entertainment|Man of Action]]'', developed the series and were executive producers on seasons 1 and 2.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://marvel.com/news/story/19551/nycc_2012_avengers_assemble_hulk_casts_announced|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121015143031/http://marvel.com/news/story/19551/nycc_2012_avengers_assemble_hulk_casts_announced|url-status=dead|archive-date=October 15, 2012|title=NYCC 2012: Avengers Assemble & Hulk Casts Announced|access-date=January 29, 2013|date=December 4, 2012|work=[[Marvel.com]]|publisher=[[Marvel Comics]]}}</ref>

The series ended on February 24, 2019, after 127 episodes over the course of five seasons, making it the longest running Marvel animated series. However, it is expected to be surpassed by [[Disney Jr.|Disney Jr]].'s ''[[Spidey and His Amazing Friends]]'' following preemptive renewals for a sixth and seventh season in June 2025 and August 2026 respectively.<ref name="Season 6 Renewal">{{Cite web |last=Lang|first=Jamie|date=June 7, 2025|title=''Spidey and his Amazing Friends'', ''Dragon Striker'' and ''Sam Witch'' Headline Disney Branded TV's Annecy Slate (EXCLUSIVE)|url=https://variety.com/2025/tv/global/disney-branded-tv-annecy-slate-1236422042/|access-date=June 7, 2025|website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Chitwood|first=Adam|date=2026-01-09|title=How Disney Kids Sensation 'Marvel's Spidey and His Amazing Friends' Reached 1 Billion Hours Streamed in 100 Episodes|url=https://www.thewrap.com/creative-content/tv-shows/spidey-and-his-amazing-friends-100th-episode-symbiote/|access-date=2026-06-01|website=TheWrap|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=‘Marvel’s Spidey And His Amazing Friends’ Renewed For Season 7 On Disney Jr. And Disney+ |url=https://press.disneyplus.com/news/disney-plus-marvels-spidey-and-his-amazing-friends-season-7-greenlight |access-date=2026-08-16 |website=Disney Plus Press |language=en}}</ref>

==Plot==
===Premise===
[[Falcon (comics)|Falcon]] is the newest member of the [[Avengers (comics)|Avengers]]. Falcon is the main eyes and ears of the viewer as he fights evil and saves the world with his teammates. The Avengers team consist of [[Iron Man]], [[Captain America]], [[Thor (Marvel Comics)|Thor]], [[Hulk]], [[Black Widow (Natasha Romanova)|Black Widow]], and [[Hawkeye (Clint Barton)|Hawkeye]].<ref name="iGN">{{cite web|url=http://www.ign.com/articles/2012/06/12/marvel-confirms-new-avengers-assemble-animated-series |title=Marvel Confirms New Avengers Assemble Animated Series |access-date=September 7, 2012 |date=June 12, 2012 |work=[[IGN]] |last=Goldman |first=Eric |archive-date=June 30, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120630084357/http://www.ign.com/articles/2012/06/12/marvel-confirms-new-avengers-assemble-animated-series |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Season 1===
When [[Red Skull]] begins dying due to his imperfect [[Super Soldier]] Serum, he allies with [[MODOK]], who uses [[Hydra (comics)|Hydra]] technology to transfer his mind to Captain America's body. After his defeat by a reassembled team of [[Avengers (comics)|Avengers]], Red Skull steals [[Iron Man]]'s armor to use its life support system. Red Skull attacks the Avengers at [[Avengers Mansion|their mansion]], which is destroyed during the fight.

To even the playing field, Red Skull brings together the [[Cabal (comics)|Cabal]], consisting of [[Attuma]], [[Dracula (Marvel Comics)|Dracula]], the [[Super-Adaptoid]], and [[Hyperion (character)|Hyperion]] (He also offers [[Doctor Doom]] a spot, but he turns down the offer). The Avengers and [[S.H.I.E.L.D.]] intercept Red Skull's transmission, learn of the Cabal's existence, and decide to remain together to battle them.

===Season 2===
The Avengers encounter Red Skull's master, [[Thanos]], who is searching for the [[Infinity Gems|Infinity Stones]].<ref name="vty">{{cite news |last1=Khatchatourian |first1=Maane |date=July 26, 2014 |title=Disney XD Renews Marvel's 'Avengers Assemble,' 'Hulk and the Agents of S.M.A.S.H.' |work=Variety |url=https://variety.com/2014/tv/news/disney-xd-renews-avengers-hulk-marvel-cartoons-1201269592/ |access-date=July 28, 2014}}</ref> They collect all five Stones and defeat Thanos, but [[Ultron]] absorbs the Stones' power. After the Avengers ultimately defeat Ultron, [[Ant-Man (Scott Lang)|Ant-Man]] becomes a new member of the team. Around the end of season two, Thanos manages to escape from a galactic prison that was meant to contain him with aid of the [[Black Order (comics)|Black Order]]. Thanos then makes one final attempt to defeat the Avengers. With help from the people of Earth, the Avengers are able to defeat Thanos and the Black Order. In the aftermath, Captain America and Iron Man plan to make Earth an "Avengers World" by recruiting other heroes.

There is also a subplot detailing the arrival of the [[Squadron Supreme]], Hyperion's old team which also consists of [[Nighthawk (Marvel Comics)|Nighthawk]], [[Power Princess]], [[Doctor Spectrum]], and [[Speed Demon (Marvel Comics)|Speed Demon]]. Believed to have died when their home planet was destroyed, they come to Earth in an attempt to conquer it. After going into hiding, the Avengers manage to defeat the Squadron Supreme by waiting until the Squadron's members divide rule of the planet's continents, then they pull a surprise attack and trap each member of the Squadron Supreme. The Squadron Supreme is imprisoned in a special section of the [[Vault (Marvel Comics)|Vault]].

===Season 3: ''Ultron Revolution''===
The Avengers are forced to cancel their expansion plans and Ant-Man leaves the team due to a lack of global threats. After dealing with A.I.M.'s Scientist Supreme, the Avengers are shocked when Ultron returns and absorbs A.I.M.'s Super-Adaptoid technology. Now Ultron is seeking revenge on the Avengers for foiling his plans with the Infinity Stones' power, while continuing his plot to replace humanity with robots. During Ultron's battle with the Avengers, Terrigen Mist is spread worldwide, activating the superpowers of humans with latent [[Inhumans|Inhuman]] genes.

One subplot details [[Helmut Zemo]], son of Captain America's old nemesis [[Heinrich Zemo]], finding a working vial of the Super-Soldier Serum and using it on himself to gain the youth and strength to face the Avengers and avenge his father's death. Zemo recruits the [[Masters of Evil]] (which consists of [[Abner Jenkins|Beetle]], [[Erik Josten|Goliath]], [[Songbird (character)|Screaming Mimi]], [[Fixer (Marvel Comics)|Fixer]], and [[Karla Sofen|Moonstone]]), who masquerade as the [[Thunderbolts (comics)|Thunderbolts]] to undermine the Avengers. However, after being saved by Captain America, Songbird eventually convinces the team to reform and turn on Zemo.

When the President signs the New Powers Act, the Avengers are given [[Truman Marsh]] as their government liaison, who replaces Hulk with [[Thunderbolt Ross|Red Hulk]]. Marsh is later revealed to be a disguise of Ultron, who mind-controls the Inhumans to attack the Avengers and possesses Iron Man. The Avengers are unable to free Iron Man from Ultron and instead place him in a dimension where technology does not work until they can find a way to drive Ultron out.

===Season 4: ''Secret Wars''===
The Avengers' plans to rescue Iron Man experience a major setback when a new version of the Cabal (consisting of [[Leader (character)|Leader]], [[Arnim Zola]], [[Enchantress (Marvel Comics)|Enchantress]], [[Executioner (character)|Executioner]], and [[Kang the Conqueror]]) transports them across time and space. Ant-Man, Captain Marvel, Ms. Marvel, Vision, and [[Hope Pym|Wasp]] work with [[Jane Foster]] to rescue them.

It is later revealed that Loki had told a powerful being known as the [[Beyonder]] of Earth. He uses the [[Bifröst]] to retrieve locations from across the universe and fuse them into the planet Battleworld. With Iron Man back with the team, the Avengers form an unlikely alliance with Loki to rebuild the Bifröst and work with Jane Foster to defeat him after he betrays the group.<ref name="Hayley-Atwell">{{cite news|last1=Awesome|first1=Amy|title=Hayley Atwell Returning As Agent Carter|url=http://comicbook.com/marvel/2016/10/08/hayley-atwell-returning-as-agent-carter/|access-date=9 October 2016|publisher=[[ComicBook.com]]|date=October 8, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cbr.com/sdcc-marvel-announces-animated-secret-wars-skottie-young-style-rocket-groot/|title=SDCC: Marvel Announces Animated "Secret Wars," Skottie Young-Style "Rocket & Groot"|date=23 July 2016|access-date=24 August 2017|archive-date=9 August 2016|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160809232938/http://www.comicbookresources.com/article/sdcc-marvel-announces-animated-secret-wars-skottie-young-style-rocket-groot}}</ref>

===Season 5: ''Black Panther's Quest''===
Following Loki's defeat, the Avengers invite [[Shuri (character)|Shuri]] to a party at their tower. However, after an attack by Atlanteans, Black Panther and Shuri set out to stop the [[Shadow Council]], including [[N'Jadaka]], [[Tiger Shark (Marvel Comics)|Tiger Shark]], [[M'Baku]], and [[Princess Zanda|Zanda]]. T'Challa tries to keep this mission a secret, telling none of the Avengers except Captain America.

The Council seeks an artifact called the Crown, which can access the memories of the deceased. Baron Zemo tries to take the Crown for himself, but it becomes unstable due to being away from [[Wakanda]]. Captain America sacrifices himself to absorb the resulting blast, with Black Widow accusing Black Panther of killing him. With the Avengers and the world against him, T'Challa frees [[Klaw (character)|Klaw]] from incarceration to learn of Killmonger's plan. He, Shuri, and Klaw enter the Hall of Royals, a burial ground for Wakanda's deceased royalty, where T'Challa researches the Council's plans. He frees Captain America, who was absorbed into the Crown.

Council member Madame Masque attacks Atlantis, triggering a war between it and Wakanda. However, Bask, the sister of Wakanda's founder Bashenga, sacrifices herself to destroy Masque's laser. She leaves for the afterlife, naming Shuri her successor, while the Crown is destroyed.

